[size=5]Soundtrack Switcher[/size]

Replace Cyberpunk 2077's story music with your own, one cue at a time. Drop a track into a folder, run one bat file, and the game plays it exactly where its own music would have played. Every cue you don't replace keeps the music CD Projekt shipped.

No REDmod. No game file is modified: everything this mod does happens through AudioXL's custom sounds and one script.

[size=4]What you get[/size]

[list]
[*]281 story cues, grouped into folders by quest, each folder named after the quest as the game names it and showing how long the original music runs.
[*]Any filename. Drop in [b]My Song.mp3[/b], [b]whatever.flac[/b], anything - .mp3, .ogg, .flac and .wav all work. Nothing has to be renamed.
[*]Loudness matched to the game's own score, so your track sits at the level the scene expects instead of blowing the dialogue away or vanishing under it. A [b]volume.txt[/b] holding e.g. -3 nudges a single cue by ear.
[*]Your track ends where the game ends its own music, with a 2 second fade, for the 255 cues of 281 that have a stop event. The 26 that do not are the credits, a few short stingers and some performance pieces; replace one of those and your track runs until the next cue starts.
[*]Looping when you want it: end the filename with [b].loop[/b] and it repeats until the game moves on.
[*]An on/off switch in Settings -> Mods, no restart needed. Off gives you the game's own music back, not silence.
[*]Story score only. Radio, combat music, district ambience and diegetic music are all left alone.
[/list]

[size=4]Requirements[/size]

[list]
[*]RED4ext
[*]redscript
[*]AudioXL 0.4.3
[*][b]AudioXL patch for Soundtrack Switcher[/b] - the second file on this page. Install it after AudioXL.
[*]Mod Settings (optional, only for the in-game on/off switch)
[/list]

[size=4]Why there are two downloads[/size]

AudioXL is what lets a mod play its own file where the game asked for its own music. It is excellent, and this mod would not exist without it. Two things it cannot do yet are things this mod needs, so the second download is AudioXL 0.4.3 with those two added.

[b]1. Ending your track where the scene ends.[/b] When the game finishes a piece of music it fires a stop event, but that event acts on the game's own music, not on a replacement. So without the patch your track does not stop when the scene does - it plays on through the next conversation and into the next quest until some other cue happens to start. The patch lets a sound name the events that end it, so your track ends on the same beat the original would have, with a fade.

[b]2. Turning replacements off in game.[/b] The on/off switch works by clearing your cues from the engine's sound table and putting them back, which is what gives you the game's own music again without a restart. AudioXL has no way to do that from outside.

[b]Do you have to install it?[/b] The mod works without it: your tracks still play. What you lose is the ending - your track runs past the scene it belongs to until another cue starts - and the in-game switch. That first one is why the patch is listed as required rather than optional.

[b]Is it safe to replace AudioXL's files?[/b] It is AudioXL 0.4.3 itself, built from DigitalVixen's own source with those additions and nothing else changed, shared under AudioXL's MIT licence with the licence included. The changes are public and have been offered to AudioXL's author. If they land in an official release, delete this download and keep official AudioXL - this mod will pick the difference up on its own. Remove the patch at any time and you are back to stock AudioXL; nothing else on your install is touched.

[size=4]Installing[/size]

[list=1]
[*]Install AudioXL, then [b]SoundtrackSwitcher[/b], then [b]AudioXL patch for Soundtrack Switcher[/b], in that order, so the patch's three files win.
[*]Open the mod's SoundtrackSwitcher folder, find the quest, find the cue.
[*]Drop your track into that cue's folder.
[*]Run [b]rescan.bat[/b] in the SoundtrackSwitcher folder. It prints what it found.
[*]Start the game.
[/list]

To undo a replacement, delete your track, run rescan.bat again, restart. A folder with no track of yours plays the game's own music.

[b]Vortex:[/b] the mod deploys into your game folder, so everything lives at [b]Cyberpunk 2077\red4ext\plugins\AudioXL\sounds\SoundtrackSwitcher[/b]. You can add rescan.bat as a tool on Vortex's dashboard (Add Tool) if you'd rather launch it from there. Your own tracks are not managed by Vortex, so updating the mod leaves them where they are. If Vortex ever asks about external changes to sounds.json or Cues.reds, keep the changed version - rescan.bat wrote those.

[b]Mod Organizer 2:[/b] run rescan.bat straight from [b]mods\SoundtrackSwitcher[/b], not through MO2, so what it writes lands in the mod folder instead of Overwrite.

[size=4]About prepare.exe[/size]

The mod ships a small tool called prepare.exe, and rescan.bat runs it on each track you add. It decodes your file, measures its loudness the way broadcast does (EBU R128), brings it to the level of the game's own music, limits the peaks so a quiet track can be raised without clipping, and writes the result next to your file.

It runs [b]before[/b] you start the game, which is the whole point: nothing is decoded, measured or converted while you are playing.

[list]
[*]It never touches the network. It reads the one file you point it at and writes one file beside it.
[*]It is not code-signed, so Windows SmartScreen may say "Windows protected your PC" the first time, and a virus scanner may flag it simply for being an unknown new binary. Both are false positives.
[*]The source is on GitHub and every release is built by GitHub Actions, so you can read exactly what it does, or build it yourself and drop your own build in.
[*]You can also just delete it. The mod still works - your files are then played as they are, without loudness matching.
[/list]

[size=4]Hearing the original first[/size]

The separate previews download renders the game's own music for every cue into that cue's folder as original.ogg, from your own installed game files. Nothing copyrighted is distributed. It needs WolvenKit's CLI and ffmpeg, which is why it is a separate optional file.

[size=4]Known issue[/size]

The game can crash while loading a save, at roughly 1 load in 9. Loading again works. The fault is inside the game's own audio code and happens with AudioXL and any custom sound registered, with or without this mod's script, so it is not something this mod can fix from the outside. It is under investigation.

[size=4]Performance, in numbers[/size]

The design rule was simple: all the expensive work happens when you run rescan.bat, none of it while you are playing.

[b]Storage[/b]
[list]
[*]The mod itself, with no tracks added: [b]2.6 MB[/b]. That is 281 cue folders, the cue data and prepare.exe.
[*]Each track you add costs your own file plus a prepared copy at [b]10.6 MB per minute[/b] of audio (uncompressed 16-bit stereo - that is what lets the game play it without decoding anything). A 3 minute track is about 32 MB.
[*]Ten replaced tracks is roughly 350 MB. Replacing every cue - 24.5 hours of music across the 278 whose length the game tells us - would be about 15 GB. Nobody is doing that, but that is the ceiling.
[*]The optional previews download renders the game's own music into the folders: about [b]1.4 GB[/b] if you render all of them. Delete them when you are done comparing.
[/list]

[b]RAM[/b]
[list]
[*]Only the cues you replaced cost anything: [b]10.6 MB per minute[/b] of replaced audio, the same as on disk. Four replaced tracks of about 3 minutes each is roughly 130 MB.
[*]That memory is a file mapping, not a heap allocation - Windows backs it with the file on disk and is free to drop those pages when something else needs the RAM, then read them back. It is the cheapest way to hold audio that must never stutter.
[*]A single file may not exceed 500 MB, which is about 47 minutes of audio.
[*]Cues you did not replace cost nothing at all. There is no table of 281 anything in memory.
[/list]

[b]CPU while playing[/b]
[list]
[*][b]Nothing of this mod runs while you play.[/b] There is no timer, no polling, no per-frame work. The game starts your track by name and the game's own stop event ends it; the mod's script only runs when a session starts and when it ends.
[*]Your track is mixed as one stereo voice with no decoding, because it was converted ahead of time. It is the cheapest kind of sound the engine has.
[*]The patch adds one atomic read per sound event the game posts, and does real work only when a track of yours is playing and the game fires the event that ends it.
[*]Nothing in the game is wrapped, replaced or hooked in script, so no other script mod pays for this one being installed.
[/list]

[b]CPU when loading[/b]
[list]
[*]Your tracks are registered once, while the game sets up audio: [b]22 ms for four tracks[/b], measured. The first launch after adding a track also reads that track off disk once.
[*]The three small script files compile with everything else redscript compiles; you will not see it in the load time.
[/list]

[b]CPU when you run rescan.bat[/b]
[list]
[*][b]0.38 seconds[/b] for a 3 minute track, measured, on one core - decode, loudness measurement, gain, limiter and write. Only new or changed tracks are processed; the rest are skipped.
[/list]

[b]GPU[/b]
[list]
[*]Nothing. The mod draws nothing at all. The on/off switch is a row in Mod Settings' existing menu.
[/list]

[size=4]Credits[/size]

